# Pipeline End Goal

Build a pipeline that can handle arbitrarily complex shots by decomposing work into the
smallest meaningful dependency-ordered units, validating each unit, and repeatedly proving
that previously accepted work still holds. Beacon Wake is a test fixture, never the design
target. The canonical rationale is in `docs/PIPELINE_END_GOAL.md`.

## Always

- Keep a layer as a department/ownership boundary; let the planner create a dynamic number
  of internal work units from the shot, references, dependencies, control families, and risk.
- Give every work unit one bounded goal, semantic mutation scope, dependencies, protected
  contracts, evidence policy, completion clause, checkpoint, and repair owner.
- Plan each work unit just in time from sealed prior outcomes instead of using one giant plan.
- Treat a wrong DAG/scope/owner as a versioned replan that preserves unaffected checkpoints,
  invalidates dependants, and marks replaced work superseded.
- Select objects and controls by semantic roles/properties, never fragile display names.
- Validate in widening rings: mutation -> unit -> interface regression -> layer regression ->
  cross-layer regression -> full-shot acceptance.
- Persist scripts, manifests, measurements, verdicts, hashes, and checkpoints on disk; do not
  rely on conversation memory or critic prose as execution authority.
- Freeze a candidate before evaluation. Parallelize isolated evidence production, then fan in
  to one ordered verdict; serialize authoritative mutation, integration, accept, and rollback.
- Make every repair transactional and restore the last accepted checkpoint on regression,
  truncation, cancellation, SDK failure, or worker failure.
- Use generic evidence primitives. Derive subjects, frames, regions, targets, stages, and
  tolerances from each shot's brief and references.
- Give autonomous blocking authority only to executable or currently qualified qualitative
  claims; otherwise improve evidence/judgement or require audited human adjudication.
- Give interaction defects a bounded coordination owner; broader balancing must replan.

## Never

- Hardcode shot names, subject names, fixed frames, fixed stage counts, or Beacon-specific
  assumptions in core pipeline code.
- Treat one layer as one giant unrestricted agent session.
- Let multiple workers mutate the same scene or script concurrently.
- Let a local fix trade away an already passing frame, unit, interface, or upstream layer.
- Repair a fault in a downstream layer when its semantic owner is known upstream.
- Ship partial/unvalidated edits or call an interrupted agent result complete.
- Let a builder silently rewrite its plan, broaden repair scope, or misclassify blocked work.
- Add compatibility fallbacks that silently revive obsolete plans, schemas, selectors, or
  execution contracts; migrations are explicit and fail closed.
- Call a mechanism general until it passes heterogeneous fixtures and injected-failure tests.
